  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Archetypometrics, a Pragmateia: Empirical Determination of the Fundamental Archetypes of Fictional Characters (2025) in Zenodo. 3272 downloads.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Archetypometrics, a Pragmateia: Empirical Determination of the Fundamental Archetypes of Fictional Characters (2025) in Zenodo is authored by Dodds, Peter Sheridan, Zimmerman, Julia Witte and Beauregard, Calla G.. Topics: Archetype, Storytelling, Computer science, Quality (philosophy) and Personality.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">No citations yet.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "ArticleStory",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 scholarly article has seen strong early uptake as a downloadable research object, with 3,272 downloads and 1,075 views recorded in DataCite. The record is still citation-quiet, with 0 citations in OpenAlex, so its influence appears to be emerging through direct readership rather than through documented scholarly reuse so far.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Dodds and colleagues present an empirical study of fictional character archetypes that spans film, television, and literature, aiming to identify a small underlying set of base forms and extend them into single, dual, and triple archetypes. The work sits at the intersection of storytelling, personality, narrative analysis, and computational approaches to culture, and it is authored by a collaboration centered at the University of Vermont, with Timothy R. Tangherlini from the University of California, Berkeley.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest sign of reach is usage: the article has attracted thousands of downloads and more than a thousand views, indicating that readers are actively accessing the work. That pattern fits the paper\u2019s design as a research resource intended to be read closely and used alongside its associated materials, rather than a work whose impact is yet visible primarily through citations.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The stewardship record also shows a linked Zenodo dataset and a related supplement, suggesting that the article is part of a broader, reusable research package. OpenAIRE has indexed multiple instances of the work, including open versions, which supports discoverability and access across repositories.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the scholarly citation record is still early. OpenAlex currently shows no citations, and scite reports no citing statements, so there is not yet evidence in this digest of documented scholarly uptake or controversy. Public discourse signals are also unavailable in the record, which means the safest reading is that the work\u2019s current footprint is driven by access and dissemination, with longer-term scholarly influence still to come.</p>",
